# Error Handling Patterns

## Backend (Express)

### Use Custom Error Classes

```javascript
// Import from server/utils/errors.js
const { AppError, NotFoundError, ValidationError } = require('../utils/errors');

// Throw typed errors
throw new NotFoundError('Customer not found');
throw new ValidationError('Invalid email format');
```

### Route Handler Pattern

```javascript
router.get('/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const result = await service.getById(req.params.id);
    if (!result) {
      throw new NotFoundError('Resource not found');
    }
    res.json({ data: result });
  } catch (error) {
    next(error); // Pass to errorHandler middleware
  }
});
```

### Error Response Format

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "NOT_FOUND",
    "message": "Customer not found",
    "status": 404
  }
}
```

## Frontend (React)

### Use Error Utilities

```typescript
// Import from src/lib/errors.ts
import { ApiError, handleApiError } from '@/lib/errors';

// In async functions
try {
  const data = await api.get('/endpoint');
} catch (error) {
  const handled = handleApiError(error);
  toast.error(handled.message);
}
```

### Component Error Boundaries

```typescript
// Wrap pages in ErrorBoundary
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<ErrorPage />}>
  <PageComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

### Loading and Error States

```typescript
const { data, error, isLoading } = useData('/endpoint');

if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
if (error) return <ErrorMessage error={error} />;
return <DataDisplay data={data} />;
```

## Logging

- Server: Use console.error with context (never in production responses)
- Client: Use error tracking service for production
- Always include request ID for correlation
